## Substantive Content

### Commencement structure (staggered, mandatory)
- Rules 1, 2, and 17–21 commence immediately on publication in the Official Gazette.
- Rule 4 commences one year after gazette publication (i.e., ~Nov 2026).
- Rules 3, 5–16, 22 and 23 commence eighteen months after gazette publication (i.e., ~May 2027).
⚠ VERIFY BEFORE PUBLISHING — exact effective dates for each rule tranche against the primary gazette text before citing in any deliverable.

### Notice requirements (Rule 3) — mandatory ("shall")
Notice given by a Data Fiduciary to a Data Principal must:
- Be presentable and understandable independently of any other information made available by the Data Fiduciary.
- Give, in clear and plain language, a fair account of the details necessary to enable the Data Principal to give specific consent.

### Consent Manager registration and obligations (Rule 4) — mandatory
- Persons meeting conditions in Part A of the First Schedule may apply to the Data Protection Board for registration as a Consent Manager.
- The Board may register, reject (with reasons), or — after an opportunity of being heard — suspend or cancel registration, and issue directions to protect Data Principals' interests.
- Consent Managers have ongoing obligations specified in Part B of the First Schedule.

### State and instrumentalities processing (Rule 5)
Separate (lighter-touch) processing pathway for personal data processed by the State and its instrumentalities for the provision or issue of subsidies, benefits, services, certificates, licences, or permits.

### Security safeguards and breach obligations (Rule 6–7) — mandatory
- Data Fiduciaries must implement "reasonable security safeguards" against personal data breach, including at minimum: encryption, obfuscation, masking, or virtual tokenisation of personal data, consistent with the Information Technology Act, 2000.
- **Intimation to Data Principal on breach (Rule 7(1))**: On becoming aware of a breach, the Data Fiduciary must, "without delay," intimate each affected Data Principal — via their registered user account or other registered communication channel — with: (a) nature/extent/timing of the breach; (b) likely consequences to the individual; (c) mitigation measures taken/underway; (d) protective steps the individual can take; and (e) contact information for a responsible person.
- **Intimation to the Data Protection Board (Rule 7(2))**: (a) "without delay," a description of the breach including nature, extent, timing, location and likely impact; (b) **within seventy-two hours** [of becoming aware, per the extracted text — ⚠ VERIFY BEFORE PUBLISHING: confirm the 72-hour trigger event and any extension provisions against the full primary text, as the extraction was truncated at this point].

